\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  stocks post biggest fall since 1998,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2  twice in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 22:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, updates closing prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jamie McGeever and Paula Laier\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA/SAO PAULO, March 9 (Reuters) - Brazilian stocks plunged more than 12% on Monday to record their biggest fall since 1998 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the real slumped to another record low, in a day of widespread fear and selling across global markets.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Bovespa index's 12.3% decline was its biggest one-day fall since October 1998 and the seventh largest ever, with preferred shares in oil giant \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  recording their steepest ever decline of 30%.\par \par \uc2 The savage sell-off came after Saudi Arabia decided to ramp up oil production as \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's supply-cut agreement with Russia collapsed, sending ripples across global financial markets already panicking about the impact of the coronavirus outbreak.\par \par \uc2 "Not a good day to be in Brazil," said Jason Vieira, chief economist at Infinity Asset Management in Sao Paulo. "Brazil is taking the hit more than anyone else, again. The local market suffers for the massive presence of \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  in the Bovespa."\par \par \uc2 "The real has probably found its floor, but it does not mean that markets won't test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again. They will," he added.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed up a $3 billion spot market auction in the morning with a $465 million sale in the afternoon, as the real slumped to a new record low just shy of 4.80 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 That marked the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market since November last year, and its largest sale of dollar reserves since 2009. It arrested the real's decline, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still lost around 2% of its value on Monday and has now depreciated by more than 15% this year.\par \par \uc2 Monetary policy director Bruno Serra said on Monday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do whatever is needed and in whatever size is needed to ensure the smooth functioning of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, adding that Brazil's $330 billion of net reserves provides a "robust insurance" against external shocks.\par \par \uc2 "The new \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach is the right one, as we're in panic mode," said a senior trader at a big bank in Sao Paulo. "It's time to provide as much liquidity as possible to contain the pressure."\par \par \uc2 The $3.465 billion spot market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps market in recent weeks worth almost $10 billion. The action prevented the real from falling below 4.80 per dollar and helped ease market volatility, if only slightly.\par \par \uc2 In stocks, trading on the B3 Bolsa Brasil Balcao was halted minutes after the open by an automatic "circuit breaker" triggered by the Bovespa's 10% fall. The exchange resumed trading and extended its decline.\par \par \uc2 The steep fall, led by energy firms, miners and banks, pushed the Bovespa deep into bear market territory, signaling a decline of 20% or more from its peak.\par \par \uc2 The index is now down 28% from its all-time high of 119,593 points on Jan. 24. In dollar terms, according to Reuters calculations, the Bovespa is now down 35.5% year to date, the steepest drop of any major equities index in the world.\par \par \uc2 Common and preferred shares in state-controlled oil company \uc2 Petroleo Brasileiro SA\uc2  sank 30%, the largest ever intraday fall. \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 ' market value lost 95 billion reais ($20 billion) compared with Friday's close.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 4.74 reais)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jamie McGeever in Brasilia, Tatiana Bautzer, Paula Laier, Jose Gomes Neto and Christian Plumb in Sao Paulo, Susan Mathew in Bangalore Editing by Steve Orlofsky and Lisa Shumaker) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-9T22:00:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200309eg3902niy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
