\par\par \b \uc2 Funds ramp up bets against Brazil's real as it has biggest weekly fall this year\b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 22:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, March 6 (Reuters) - Funds and speculators on U.S. futures markets increased their bearish bets against the Brazilian real for the seventh week in a row, data showed on Friday, the same day the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  registered its biggest weekly fall since November.\par \par \uc2 A $2-billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps market from \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped pull the real up from a fresh record low, but it still ended the week down 3.4% as traders moved to price in a cut in interest rates later this month.\par \par \uc2 That brought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sales of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps contracts in recent weeks to $9.5 billion, a concerted effort to slow or reverse the real's decline which accelerated amid a rapidly deteriorating economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 The latest \uc2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2  data on Friday showed that funds and speculators on U.S. futures markets are now holding their largest net short real position this year.\par \par \uc2 To go short a financial asset is to effectively bet that it will decline in value.\par \par \uc2 Funds' net short position as of March 3 grew to 42,858 contracts from 36,987 contracts the week before, CFTC data showed. That is the seventh week in a row of rising net short positions, which are now near last December's record 51,274.\par \par \uc2 But those figures were for the week to Tuesday, when the real traded as strong as 4.45 per dollar before sliding to as weak as 4.67 per dollar on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Traders said a rate cut at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next policy meeting on March 17-18 is all but guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a big problem, so now is not the time to cut rates," said a senior trader in Sao Paulo, noting that lower rates will likely put the real under even more downward pressure.\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's emergency rate cut this week, \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was monitoring financial conditions as coronavirus fears tanked local markets and triggered a wave of downward economic growth forecast revisions.\par \par \uc2 Economists at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  on Friday became the latest to lower their 2020 growth outlook, to 1.7%. The government will lower its forecast next week, but not below 2%, Economy Minister \uc2 Paulo Guedes\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jamie McGeever Editing by Alistair Bell) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-6T22:56:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200306eg3604dpt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
