\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  says  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  again if required\b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2020\par 23:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds market background on inflation expectations, paragraph 7)\par \par \uc2 By Gabriel Ponte\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Feb 18 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, \uc2 Roberto Campos Neto\uc2 , said on Tuesday the bank is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to address illiquidity or excessive market moves in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, as it did last week.\par \par \uc2 Answering questions from lawmakers in Brasilia, \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  emphasized that the real is a floating exchange rate, and that unlike in the past, its current weakness has not been accompanied by rising risk premiums and falling stock markets.\par \par \uc2 "We are calm. Obviously, whenever we identify a lack of liquidity, excessive moves, or Brazil is becoming detached from its peers or inflation expectations are being affected, then we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The bank waded into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market twice last week, its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in almost three months, selling $1 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps contracts both Thursday and Friday as the real slumped to a record low below 4.38 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 That had taken its losses against the dollar so far this year to almost 10%, making it one of the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted.\par \par \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  told lawmakers that the real's current weakness has been a function of falling commodity prices, falling Brazilian interest rates and the dollar's global strength. Crucially, it has not fueled inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest weekly "FOCUS" survey of economists shows that inflation expectations are falling, not rising. The average forecast for this year is now 3.22%, well below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4.00% target.\par \par \uc2 "Interest rate differentials and widening growth expectation differentials between the U.S. and other countries has strengthened the dollar," \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  said. "So the real's depreciation now is very different from those in the past."\par \par \uc2 The real registered its record low close against the dollar on Tuesday close to 4.36, as the impact of last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  faded and the 2020 growth outlook darkened further.\par \par \uc2 In an earlier address to lawmakers, \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  said that the more independent a \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is, the lower inflation is, adding that Brazil is the only country in the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  major economies without \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  autonomy. (Reporting by Gabriel Ponte in Brasilia Writing by Jamie McGeever Editing by Leslie Adler and Matthew Lewis) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-2-18T23:12:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200218eg2i03unp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
