\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's reluctant  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  may give brief respite\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2020\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Feb 14 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took aim at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators on Thursday, but its first market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in almost three months appeared to be a reluctant one and the effects may be short-lived given a dimming economic outlook for 2020.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  waded into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives market selling $1 billion worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, as the real's lurch to another record low against the dollar gathered pace and threatened to bring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's year-to-date losses to a whopping 10%.\par \par \uc2 The sale of contracts expiring in March, October and December triggered a sharp reversal in the dollar to as low as 4.31 reais from over 4.38 reais, although at the close of trading it had settled around 4.34 reais.\par \par \uc2 It was the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps market in a year and a half, and will be repeated on Friday. In November, policymakers sold dollars in the spot market to address what they called a lack of liquidity.\par \par \uc2 "It is interesting that they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  via swaps, which is normally aimed at taming speculation. The market was testing the limits and they found one," said Drausio Giacomelli, head of emerging market research at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "But the underlying view at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy Ministry is that the weak real is okay," he said, referring to comments late on Wednesday by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Roberto Campos Neto\uc2  and Economy Minister \uc2 Paulo Guedes\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a TV interview with GloboNews broadcast just before midnight on Wednesday, \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  said several times that the exchange rate is a floating exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Asked to comment specifically on earlier remarks by Guedes that showed his comfort with a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which might encourage further selling at the market open on Thursday, \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  repeated that the real is a floating exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The clear implication was that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's preference was not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market if it could help it, traders said, despite pressure to do so as the real slumped to record lows day after day.\par \par \uc2 Unsurprisingly, the laissez-faire double whammy from \uc2 Campos Neto\uc2  and Guedes unleashed another wave of selling. The real crashed through 4.38 per dollar for the first time ever, taking its losses against the greenback so far this year to 9%.\par \par \uc2 SHORT-TERM STABILITY\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Citi noted that November's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market steadied the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a month. Following Thursday's move in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps market, the real "should at least stabilize and possibly move back below 4.30 again in due course," they wrote in a client note.\par \par \uc2 But if the November playbook is any guide, the respite may be short-lived. The real's rebound of almost 7% in the last five weeks of 2019 was swiftly erased by the near-10% slump in the first six weeks of this year.\par \par \uc2 A sustained rally may also be unlikely if upcoming data continue to show economic growth losing steam and lead to more downward revisions to 2020 forecasts.\par \par \uc2 With economic signals largely pointing in one direction, markets are showing little of the whipsaw trading that would justify heavier \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth sharp swings.\par \par \uc2 Market volatility remains relatively contained, picking up in recent weeks but still nowhere near the peaks of August last year, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling dollar reserves for the first time in over a decade.\par \par \uc2 Even after that, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are a sizeable $360 billion, but there are few signs that policymakers want to tap that stockpile.\par \par \uc2 "Nobody's worried about the exchange rate," Treasury Secretary Mansueto Almeida told Reuters in an interview on Wednesday, stressing that \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy lies solely with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "(It) would be worrying if we had a very wide current account deficit and were unable to finance it, and we had very low \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. None of that is the case today," he said.\par \par \uc2 The current account deficit approaching 3% of gross domestic product is adequately funded by foreign direct investment worth over 4% of gross domestic product. (Reporting by Jamie McGeever Editing by Leslie Adler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-2-14T08:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200214eg2e00u65\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
