\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Foreign Reserves Reduced With  \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2019\par 14:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0823 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves shrunk by $3.5B in November, to $366.4B, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says. The bank sold $4.5B last month to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets amid fears the US-China trade spat would end up hurting emerging markets. The dollar sale was partially offset by a $1.6B income with repurchase contracts. Brazil had for years avoided tapping its reserves. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the goal is to placate unusual volatility and not to artificially strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The real traded today at 4.06 per dollar, stronger than 4.26 per dollar late last month. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0800 ET - Spanish economic activity remained elevated in 2019 but a weaker domestic labor market and global environment will slow activity in 2020, ING economist \uc2 Steven Trypsteen\uc2  says. Spain's unemployment rate stopped declining at the beginning of 2019, currently standing at 14%, much higher than the 8% seen before the financial crisis, Trypsteen says. A weaker labor market hasn't had "a strong impact" on consumption, but consumer confidence started to drop over summer and is now at its lowest level since January 2015, which will negatively impact consumption, he says. Aside from domestic issues, a weaker external environment will also hurt export growth, according to ING. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0746 ET - The U.K. economy is on track to register the weakest performance in a decade for 2019, despite an upward revision to third-quarter gross domestic product, EY Item Club says. GDP growth should be "limited" at 1.3%, down from 1.4% in the 2018, \uc2 EY\uc2 's \uc2 Howard Archer\uc2  says. "This assumes fourth quarter growth of 0.1% quarter-on-quarter at best." The economy may see a "spike in activity" in the first quarter but "we suspect it will be unable to build on this" as Brexit uncertainties are "likely to remain significant through 2020." The final estimate for third-quarter GDP was revised to 0.4% quarter-on-quarter from 0.3% previously. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 ET - Communication around monetary policy will be a key skill for Andrew Bailey as Governor of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , as henavigates political uncertainty and a late-cycle global economy, says \uc2 Oliver Blackbourn\uc2 , portfolio manager on the U.K.-based Multi-Asset team at Janus Henderson Investors. "With monetary policy still operating at emergency levels 10 years after the great financial crisis, the new governor is going to need to get creative in the next economic downturn," he says. "Any move to support significantly expanded fiscal policy will bring questions about the Bank's independence," for which "communication is likely to be a key skill." (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0722 ET - Julius Baer reduces the GDP forecast for Spain to 1.7% for 2020, driven by the "perspective of a weak government" which is "unfavorable to business freedom." Private consumption has been below expectations and the possibility of an rise in taxes could worsen this trend, the bank says, although it expects wage increases, a decline in the households debt ratio and favorable financing conditions to support domestic demand. Julius Baer has also downwardly revised its Spanish inflation forecast, expecting 1.8% in 2020. Julius Baer forecasts 2% GDP growth for Spain in 2019, reducing the growth differential and coming closer to the 1.1% growth rate of other eurozone countries. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0713 ET - Scope Ratings says the challenges of the Spanish economy will persist and forecasts a slowdown in growth to between 1.5% and 1.7% over the coming years, from 2% expected in 2019. According to Scope Ratings, the underlying challenges of the Spanish economy are elevated public debt estimated at around 96% for 2019, a structural unemployment rate of around 14%, pervasive use of temporary contracts and youth unemployment more than double the eurozone average. However, Spain will be above the eurozone's average growth of 1%. "Domestic demand, underpinned by improvements in the financial position of households and firms, as well as easy financial conditions stemming from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's accommodative monetary policy, will continue to sustain a growing economy," Scope Ratings says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 ET - Diverging growth and inflation are likely to create a "difficult situation" for the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's new Governor Andrew Bailey, says \uc2 Oliver Blackbourn\uc2 , portfolio manager on the U.K.-based multi-asset team at Janus Henderson Investors. "The consequences of leaving the EU will be at the top of the new governor's in-tray, especially as the consequences could be to send growth and inflation in opposing directions." Further weakness in the pound after a short-lived post-election bounce on a Conservative government majority will stoke inflation at a time when uncertainty is likely to weigh on U.K. growth, and subsequently sterling, he says. The BOE can only really target growth or inflation at a time, he says. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0647 ET - \uc2 Banca March\uc2  expects the Spanish economy to grow 2% this year, slowing down to 1.6% in 2020 driven by "a drop in confidence." Although the Spanish economy will grow supported by domestic demand, there will be a reduction in the growth differential with other economies in the eurozone, \uc2 Banca March\uc2  says. Given a context of less optimism among consumers and companies, \uc2 Banca March\uc2  worries about a rise on the savings rate or a lack of dynamism in employment. Amid the uncertainty surrounding the formation of a new government, "it will be difficult to achieve the deficit target," Joan Bonet says, market strategy director for \uc2 Banca March\uc2  . "High debt and high deficit are harmful friends for economic growth," Bonet says. (carlos.perea@dowjones.com) (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 ET - A relatively low level of \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  borrowing in November shows public finances remain in good health despite sluggish economic growth this year, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. Official data Friday showed the government borrowed GBP5.6 billion in November, above the GBP5.0 billion expected by economists in a WSJ poll but down from GBP11.2 billion in October. Full-year borrowing will total GBP42.4 billion--GBP5.2 billion below the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2  's March forecast--which provides scope for "modest fiscal stimulus" next year, Pantheon economist \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2  says. Conservatives promised to turn on the fiscal spending tap ahead of its landslide victory in the Dec. 12 U.K. general election. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0611 ET - The \uc2 Spanish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says available data points to a 0.4% GDP growth in the fourth quarter, with a total growth of 2% in 2019. "The pace of output in the Spanish economy, which the downturn in the external environment has also affected, has stabilized in the final stretch of the year," the \uc2 Bank of Spain\uc2  says. Though the Spanish manufacturing sector has also been affected by the slowdown on global demand, industrial activity has been more favorable in Spain than in other eurozone countries, where the unfavorable trend in manufacturing extended to the services sector, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says. The easing in activity in 2019 is also due partly to the loss of momentum of domestic demand, but "continuing easy financing conditions" will support household and business consumption and investment, it says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0610 ET - The upward revision to third-quarter U.K. gross domestic product Friday does not change the outlook for an economic slowdown, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. Recent data suggests the economy "may have done no better than flatline" in the fourth quarter, \uc2 Andrew Wishart\uc2  at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. "And whatever happens, Brexit uncertainty looks set to persist." The final GDP estimate for third quarter was raised to 0.4% quarter-on-quarter from 0.3% previously, surprising economists in a WSJ survey who expected an unchanged reading. The annual growth rate was also unexpectedly lifted to 1.1% from 1.0%. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0609 ET - London financial chiefs have welcomed the appointment of Andrew Bailey to replace \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  as the governor of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 . Miles Celic, head of lobby group TheCityUK said Bailey's strong reputation for being future-focused, his commitment to open global markets and his focus at the \uc2 Financial Conduct Authority\uc2  for combining fairness and sustainability will be hugely beneficial when he assumes the role of Governor of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 . "His appointment sends a clear signal about the positive future direction of our \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we look forward to working with him in the New Year," he says. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 20, 2019 08:23 ET (13:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020191220efck001k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
