\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-U.S.-China trade optimism lift Latam markets;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  helps  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's real\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2019\par 16:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Agamoni Ghosh\par     Sept 5 (Reuters) - Latin American assets moved higher on\par Thursday after the United States and China agreed to resume\par trade negotiations, raising hopes of resolving a long-standing\par trade dispute that has caused a global economic slowdown.   \par     MSCI's index of Latin American stocks rose\par 2.4%, led by gains on Brazil's Bovespa index.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in commodity export-oriented Latin American\par economies that have been battered by the U.S.-China trade war\par rose broadly against a soft dollar, but underperformed compared\par with emerging market peers in other regions.    \par     "Political-economic disruptions to global markets via the\par U.S-China trade dispute have pushed weakness in EM," \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD\par Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said in a note. \par     "Until we see stabilization in growth momentum and financial\par market volatility, it is unlikely we can make a case for strong\par performance against the USD in the Latam space," they said.\par     The Bovespa rose 1.9%, aided by gains among\par financials and energy stocks. The real, which has lost\par more than 5% of its value this year, moved 0.3% higher on the\par day as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stabilized the market.  \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Roberto Campos Neto\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Thursday\par that inflation in Brazil was well-anchored over the short-,\par medium- and long-term horizons, giving policymakers room to\par reduce interest rates further.\par     \par     ARGENTINA CRISIS       \par     The Argentine peso held steady, supported by capital\par controls imposed by Latin America's third-largest economy\par earlier this week, which came after the government announced its\par plans to "re-profile" some $100 billion in debt.\par     The peso has been battered since President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mauricio Macri\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par suffered a thumping defeat in primaries at the hands of Alberto\par Fernandez. \par     Some of Argentina's largest creditors, who met informally\par this week according to a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Financial Times\f28 \uc2 \f2  report, are said to\par have concluded that negotiations with Macri's government would\par be futile if Fernandez ultimately comes to power in October.\par     "Any deal could be torn up by the new government, so that\par suggests that we're in a period of limbo until the new\par government comes into power,\u8221\'20\'1D said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Edward Glossop\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Latin America\par economist at Capital Economics.  \par \par   Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 14:45 GMT\par  Stock indexes                             daily % change\par                            Latest         \par  MSCI Emerging Markets           1003.74             1.33\par                                           \par  MSCI LatAm                      2670.11             2.27\par                                           \par  Brazil Bovespa                103052.65             1.83\par                                           \par  Mexico IPC                     42619.86              0.7\par                                           \par  Chile IPSA                      4812.72             1.16\par                                           \par  Argentina MerVal               25956.76             5.39\par                                           \par  Colombia IGBC                  12682.58            -0.03\par                                           \par                                                          \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                 daily % change\par                                   Latest  \par  Brazil real                      4.0815             0.37\par                                           \par  Mexico peso                     19.6705             0.22\par                                           \par  Chile peso                        719.1             0.47\par                                           \par  Colombia peso                   3371.95             0.63\par  Peru sol                         3.3658             0.36\par                                           \par  Argentina peso                  55.9500             0.13\par  (interbank)                              \par                                           \par  \par \par  (Reporting by Agamoni Ghosh in Bengaluru; Additional reporting\par by Ambar Warrick; Editing by Dan Grebler)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-9-5T15:46:15.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190905ef9502p2h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
