\par\par \b \uc2 * \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Announced  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Steadies  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2019\par 16:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 15 Aug 2019 10:46 ET  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Announced \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Steadies \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Paulo Trevisani\par \par \uc2 BRAZILIA--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadied Thursday after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it will sell dollars from its vast reserves next week for the first time in 10 years, following disturbing news from some of its largest trade partners.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real was trading at 4.02 per dollar, slightly stronger than 4.04 per dollar the day before but still weaker than 3.94 per dollar it closed at Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will sell $3.84 billion between Aug. 21 and 29, roughly 1% of its total reserves of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while also offering reverse-swap contracts that amount to future purchases of dollars. The final result, the bank said in a statement, will leave the reserves' balance stable. It was $388.1 billion as of June.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the auctions will change the exchange rate in any meaningful way, but could reduce volatility," said economist \uc2 Andre Perfeito\uc2  from Necton brokerage firm.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened after leftist candidate Alberto Fernandez won primary presidential elections in Argentina on Sunday, sparking fears a new government to be elected in October could hurt trade between the two largest economies in South America.\par \par \uc2 This year Brazil is carrying a $3.9 billion trade surplus with Argentina as of July. Argentina is its third largest trade partner, after China and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Disappointing economic growth in China, with which Brazil had a $17.4 billion trade surplus as of July, also added to concerns about Latin America's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled weekly by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast Brazil's economy will grow a meager 0.8% this year, while having a strong $52 billion trade surplus.\par \par \uc2 The weakening real comes amid a global rise in the U.S. dollar's value as investors concerned with slow growth and trade disputes seek the greenback's safety, economists say.\par \par \uc2 "Risk of recession in Brazil and signs of global slowdown are causing a flight to quality," said economist and former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  director \uc2 Alexandre Schwartsman\uc2 . "Everything is happening at the same time."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schwartsman said uncertainty caused by both global and domestic developments makes Brazilian investors hold on to their U.S. dollars. That, in turn, deepens a shortage of dollars that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to solve.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move may also reflect a historical reduction in Brazil's borrowing costs, according to economist Carlos Kawall, from Safra bank. He said short-term interest rates at 6% and likely to fall later this year mean Brazilian corporations now find it cheaper to pay down their dollar debt and borrow in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead, increasing demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I don't see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move as a simple reaction to recent developments," he said. "It's addressing a shortage of dollars that's caused by a benign factor, which is low interest rates."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's benchmark Selic rate was 14.25% as of October 2016, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began cutting it to the historic low of 6% reached earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Trevisani at Paulo.Trevisani@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 15, 2019 10:46 ET (14:46 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190815ef8f00259\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
