\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Markets Expect  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2018\par 13:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1137 GMT - Brazilian markets are bracing for \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets today after governor \uc2 Ilan Goldfajn\uc2  said late yesterday that it's ready to deploy $20B or more in derivatives investors may use to hedge their bets on the Brazilian real. Goldfajn also said the bank may tap Brazil's $380B reserves in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to supply greenbacks. He said, however, that interest rates, now at historic low of 6.5%, won't be used to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The real weakened to 3.96 per dollar yesterday from 3.15 per dollar in January amid an emerging-market sellout. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1033 GMT - The \uc2 European Union\uc2  is unlikely to accept the U.K.'s proposed backstop solution to the Irish border, says Davy Research. While negotiations for a post-Brexit deal with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  are ongoing, the U.K.'s proposed solution is to keep the country in the customs union for a period of time after Brexit in order to avoid a hard border between the Irish Republic and Northern Ireland. The problem, says Davy, is that allowing the entirety of the U.K. access to the customs union violates the \uc2 EU\uc2  's prohibition on cherry-picking the single market's four freedoms--free movement of goods, capital, services and labor. As such the deal is unlikely to be accepted by Brussels.(carlo.martuscelli@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1015 GMT - Markets will closely monitor every step of the new Italian government and in this respect, it is important to look at the strengths and weaknesses of the Italian economy, says Deutsche Asset Management. Referring to the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  's "Doing Business" report in which Italy ranks 46th out of 190 countries, Deutsche AM says Italy scores poorly in categories such as paying taxes, enforcing contracts and granting loans, thus measures to improve the tax system, the legal system and lending are urgently needed. Italy performed well in cross-border trade where, together with a few other European countries, it ranks first globally. "It is therefore not surprising that Italy has achieved a current account surplus of over EUR45 billion over the past 12 months," Deutsche AM says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0927 GMT - Denmark's need for debt issuance is falling due to strong public finances but continuing bond issuance is crucial to maintain a well-functioning debt market, says \uc2 Nordea\uc2  . Against this backdrop, \uc2 Nordea\uc2  expects the suspension of the treasury bills program, while government bond issuance should focus on two- and 10-year nominal bonds as well as on-the-run inflation-linked bonds, says analyst Maria Holm Rasmussen. "We believe that it will be difficult for the \uc2 Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce issuance materially," she says, adding that, therefore, any reduction will be small when compared to the level needed to maintain bond market liquidity. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT - Taiwan's better-than-expected trade data for May suggest global tech demand is still robust despite the risk of escalating U.S-China trade tensions. Exports grew a faster-than-expected 14% in May from a year earlier, accelerating from April's 10% and notching a 3rd-consecutive month of double-digit gains. Taiwan plays a key role in the global tech supply chain, with it home to major \uc2 Apple\uc2  suppliers \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor\uc2  and Hon Hai. The island's exports are closely watched as a gauge of tech-cycle strength. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0830 GMT - April's CPI in China is projected to have been repeated in May as pork prices continued weighing on food prices and rising fuel costs drove up non-food items, economists say. The median forecast of 11 economists polled by WSJ is for CPI to have remained at 1.8%. But PPI is seen having climbed to 3.9% from April's 3.4% increase from a year earlier. Predicted to have pressured that metric was rising commodities prices. The inflation data are due Saturday. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0817 GMT - The \uc2 PBoC\uc2  is liable to make either 1 or 2 further cuts to lenders' reserve-requirement ratios in 2H to boost liquidity amid slower capital inflows, say Macquarie economists. In a strong-dollar environment, "capital inflows could slow or turn into outflows," they note. Nonetheless, the economists don't expect any large outflows, and they see little room for the yuan to depreciate further against the dollar. It sees the greenback ending the year at CNY6.5; it's currently at CNY6.41. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT - \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials' insistence that the exit plan from the asset purchases will be discussed in June is a "clear warning against speculation that the exit will be delayed due to Italian uncertainty," says Eric Oynoyan, rates strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  . On Wednesday, \uc2 ECB\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Peter Praet\uc2  sent a strong signal that the \uc2 ECB\uc2  could decide as soon as next week to wind down its EUR30 billion per month bond-buying program. Oynoyan adds that the recovery in Italian debt over the past week means that "fundamentals are now back in the driving seat for bond-market valuation." (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0802 GMT - A one-notch downgrade of Italy's credit rating by \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  is priced in, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's rates strategist Adam Kurpiel. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  next scheduled review date of Italy is on Sept. 7 but Kurpiel says that "it remains a sad reality that [it] could possibly happen before." \uc2 Moody's\uc2  placed Italy's "Baa2" rating on review for downgrade in late May. Kurpiel says that the most likely scenario is a one-notch downgrade to "Baa3" with a stable outlook, while a move accompanied with a negative outlook would be "disruptive." The "Baa3" rating is the lowest one in investment grade. Both S&P Global Ratings and \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  have currently "BBB" rating with stable outlook on Italy but "there could also be surprises" from them, Kurpiel adds. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0755 GMT - Spreads between the yields on French government bonds, or OATs, and German Bunds have the potential to narrow, say \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  rates strategists. "We are constructive on OATs and see room for further tightening against Bunds," they say. The 10-year French-German yield spread is 36 basis points, while the year-to-date bottom was at around 22 basis points in mid-April. By contrast, the 10-year Italian BTP-Bund spread "does not have much room to narrow" below \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's fair-value band of around 200 bps to 250 bps. The 10-year Italian-German yield spread is at 260 bps, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0743 GMT - Tumult related to Italy, trade and geopolitics resulted in net inflows for money-market funds tracked by data firm EPFR hitting levels last seen in October 2013. The global total was more than $55 billion for the week ended Wednesday, including more than $45 billion in US funds. But folks didn't totally eschew risk as May turned into June. Asia equity funds excluding Japan saw the biggest net inflows among regions globally and Chinese ones logged an 11-straight week of inflows amid the MSCI's inclusion of A-shares into some of its indexes. (joanne.chiu@wsj.com; @joannechiuhk)\par \par \uc2 0729 GMT - Portugal's government-bond auction window is open next Wednesday and the country's debt agency is therefore expected to announce an auction later Friday. Portugal's debt agency earmarks the second, fourth and fifth Wednesday of a month for bond auctions and it usually the picks the first opportunity, while it makes the auction announcement on the Friday before. Upcoming redemptions of Portuguese bonds also make an auction probable next week with almost EUR8 billion in backflows to investors, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategist Michael Leister. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 08, 2018 07:37 ET (11:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180608ee68001ik\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
