\par\par \uc2 CENFENG\par \b \uc2 The peso is around 21 per dollar; Where will it go?\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2018\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 In seven weeks, the weight has lost 13.5% against the dollar. The exchange rate was at 17.99 pesos per day on April 17.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday cerr \u243\'00\'F3 at 20.42, in the interbank market. In the windows of banks reached up to 20.90 per day.\par \par \uc2 How much can the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciate? Most analysts are inclined to think that the exchange rate will close down. the year in a range of 20 to 22 pesos per dollar. The most optimistic dare to put it in less than 20. The pessimists venture into a figure above 22. The forecast is complicated, among other things, because there are many factors that can move the exchange rate. To begin with, we have three sources of uncertainty: the negotiations of the FTA; the US-Mexico relationship in the Trump era and the electoral process in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Each of these three factors is like a coin in the air. It can fall on the face or the cross. We can close 2018 with the cleared road for a modernized \uc2 NAFTA\uc2 , but we could also wake up, a day of news, with the news that Trump "denounces" \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  and gives us six months to prepare for the end of the agreement. With Mr. Trump, we do not know what. will do. Enter the category of geopolitical risk.\par \par \uc2 In the case of the Mexican election, the best or worst scenario does not necessarily have to do with the triumph or defeat of a candidate. We can speak of a very simple scenario if after July 2 we find ourselves in a scenario of political polarization and with an elected president unable to send messages of tranquility to the markets ... and to the population. Mexican, including the 50 million workers, the 30 million households and the 4 million micro, small, medium and large entrepreneurs.\par \par \uc2 I have talked about three factors because they are the most relevant, but they are not the only ones that affect the exchange rate. Take yesterday's journey as an example. The weight went backwards more than 2%, among other things, as a speculative attack against the Brazilian real reacts.\par \par \uc2 \u191\'00\'BF The Mexican peso loses value because of something that happened. in Brazil? It sounds weird, because between the City of Mexico and S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo there are 7,427 kilos. Because between Mexico and Brazil, there is little commercial exchange. It sounds weird but it is possible. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with 3,000 million dollars to protect the real, provoked; a speculative wave that arrived up to our country. I'm affected by Mexico what is there? happening in Brazil because investors are smelling risk in emerging markets and want to seek refuge in developed countries.\par \par \uc2 The volatility that we have seen in the last seven weeks will continue. for a while. The "fear index" of the Mexican peso produced by Quanta Shares records levels of 21%. This indicates that investors are nervous and express it buying options on the Chicago Stock Exchange.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday was Brazil. Two weeks ago it was Italy. In 10 days it can be anywhere. There are many red lights on the world map. This affects us because we are relatively vulnerable. Our condition does not have to do with the level of reserves of the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 . It is high: 173.250 million dollars. More good with thefact that we have three coins in the air. TLC, Trump and elections. They are floating and can fall on heads or tails.\par \par \uc2 lmgonzalez@eleconomista.com.mx\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020180719ee680078n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict