\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Brazil's Temer sees no  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  crisis; cenbank vows to keep acting\b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2018\par 02:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background, Temer comments)               \par \par \uc2 By Mateus Maia and Lisandra Paraguassu               \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, June 7 (Reuters) - Brazilian President \uc2 Michel Temer\uc2  said on Thursday there was no risk of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in Latin America's largest economy despite sharp falls in the exchange rate, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief pledged to maintain the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.               \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real plummeted to its weakest level in more than two years on Thursday, at 3.9671 to the dollar, amid concerns over the country's fiscal outlook and uncertainty surrounding October's wide-open presidential election to select a successor to market-friendly Temer.               \par \par \uc2 Brazil's benchmark Bovespa equities index also fell, declining as much as 6.5 percent before recovering some ground.               \par \par \uc2 Investor confidence in Temer's government was shaken after it was forced to reinstate costly fuel subsidies following a strike by truckers that brought the country almost to a halt.               \par \par \uc2 The move prompted the resignation of the head of state oil company \uc2 Petroleo Brasileiro SA\uc2  and stirred concerns among investors over government finances as the economy struggles to gain momentum after a painful recession. Temer had made tackling Brazil's hefty budget deficit a priority for his center-right administration.               \par \par \uc2 "We are very committed to a budgetary adjustment. The fiscal question is completely under control," Temer told state television TV Brasil. "There is no risk (of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis). It is normal: The dollar is swinging around a lot."               \par \par \uc2 Temer said part of the strong moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was due to the prospect of higher interest rates in the United States, which has affected several emerging market economies. He acknowledged that Brazil's presidential election was also concerning investors, but said that whomever was victorious would need to continue with his government's fiscal reforms.               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Ilan Goldfajn\uc2 , meanwhile, pledged to continue providing liquidity to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate markets "as long as needed."               \par \par \uc2 Goldfajn told a news conference that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would offer up to $20 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps through the end of next week. He did not exclude using \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spot market.               \par \par \uc2 "All instruments are valid," he said. "For now, it seems liquidity is needed through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps. If the situation changes, if there is demand, we may use reserves."               \par \par \uc2 Goldfajn ruled out any extraordinary meeting of the bank's monetary policy committee to take action on interest rates, noting that inflation expectations remain within the bank's targets.               \par \par \uc2 "I would like to stress that monetary policy is separated from exchange rate policy. There is not a mechanical relationship between them," he said. "Monetary policy...will not be used to control exchange rates."               \par \par \uc2 He said the committee would analyze at its next monetary policy meeting whether conditions remain the same as in May, when it unexpectedly decided to keep rates stable at 6.5 percent.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-6-8T01:10:22.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180608ee68000ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
