\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil currency \highlight\uc2  firms after  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2017\par 00:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     MEXICO CITY, April 17 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real firmed\par on Monday as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while the\par view that the government will stick to its plans to push a\par pension overhaul also helped to boost the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as well as\par local stocks.\par     The real firmed 1.35 percent against the dollar to\par its strongest level in nearly two weeks after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par sold $800 million worth of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps, which function like\par dollar sales to investors for future delivery, to roll over\par contracts expiring next month.\par     Should the bank maintain that pace of sales daily until the\par end of the month, it will fully roll over $6.4 billion worth of\par swaps set to mature on March 2. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  currently holds\par around $17.7 billion worth of swaps on its balance sheet.\par     Meanwhile, lawmakers who attended a meeting with Brazilian\par President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michel Temer\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Sunday said he was sticking with his\par plans to push the pension overhaul through Congress by July.\par \par     The proposed pension reform is a cornerstone of Temer's plan\par to restore fiscal discipline and bolster investor confidence to\par spark a recovery, but it remains unpopular among Brazilians,\par many of whom would have to work longer before retirement.\par     Brazil's benchmark Bovespa stock index jumped 2.5\par percent on Monday, the biggest gain among the region's bourses.\par     Despite the gains in Brazil, escalating tension between the\par United States and North Korea, depressed investor appetite for\par higher-yielding, emerging market assets.\par     A day after a failed missile test by North Korea, U.S. Vice\par President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mike Pence\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Monday warned that recent U.S. military\par strikes in Syria and Afghanistan show that the resolve of\par President Donald Trump should not be tested.\par     The news led other Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to seesaw, with\par the Mexican peso slipping from a five-month peak,\par though Chile's peso followed copper prices higher.\par \par   Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 2000 GMT:\par  Stock indexes                  Latest      Daily   YTD pct\par                                              pct    change\par                                            change   \par  MSCI Emerging Markets             963.33      0.3    11.38\par                                                     \par  MSCI LatAm                      2,655.15      1.9    11.32\par  Brazil Bovespa                 64,394.78      2.5     6.92\par  Mexico IPC                     49,007.81     0.11     7.37\par  Chile IPSA                      4,860.48     0.04    17.08\par  Chile IGPA                     24,376.56     0.07    17.57\par  Argentina MerVal               20,723.16    -0.42    22.49\par  Colombia IGBC                  10,174.39    -0.74     0.46\par  Venezuela IBC                  47,329.03     0.64    49.28\par                                                            \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                      Latest      Daily   YTD pct\par       