\par\par \b \uc2 AGLOAN-Periodic Updates on the Grains, Livestock Futures Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2017\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2017, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 15022017\par \par \uc2 As we near the close, March corn is up 4 cents, March soybeans are up 14 cents, and March Chicago wheat is up 3 1/4 cents.\par \par \uc2 Along with soybeans higher price, March soybean meal is trading up $5.70 after Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s larger-than-expected crush total from NOPA. The March U.S. dollar index is quiet, down 0.06, but Brazil\u8217\'20\'19s real is up 1% to its highest price in over a year after \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, reported RioTimesOnline.com. A more expensive real helps encourage U.S. grain exports.\par \par \uc2 Posted 11:48 -- March corn is up 4 1/4 cents, March soybeans are up 12 3/4 cents, and March Chicago wheat is up 3 cents. The \uc2 National Oilseed Processors Association\uc2  estimated 160.62 million bushels of U.S. soybeans crushed in January, up 6.8% from a year ago and more than expected. Soybean oil stocks totaled 1.629 billion pounds at the end of January, up 6.7% from a year ago and also more than expected. March soybean meal is up $5.30 while bean oil is up 0.02 -- a strong (bullish) show for meal demand.\par \par \uc2 Posted 09:39 -- March corn is up 1 1/2 cents, March soybeans are up 7 3/4 cents, and March Chicago wheat is up 2 3/4 cents. All three are higher with modest commercial buying seen in the soy complex ahead of Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s crush report from the \uc2 National Oilseed Processors\u8217\'20\'19 Association\uc2 . The \uc2 U.S. Energy Information Administration\uc2  said last week\u8217\'20\'19s ethanol production slipped from 1.055 to 1.040 million barrels a day while inventory climbed from 22.1 to 22.5 million barrels. The production pace is still high enough to be considered slightly bullish for corn prices.\par \par \uc2 Posted 08:34 -- After the 8:30 open, March corn is down 2 1/4 cents, March soybeans are down 3 1/2 cents, and March Chicago wheat is down 2 1/4 cents. Grains are off to a quiet start and leaning lower while there are no significant weather threats to mention in South America. Also pressuring grains, the U.S. dollar index is up 0.38 after the Labor Department said consumer prices were up 2.5% in January from a year ago, more than expected due to a jump in energy prices. U.S. retail sales were up .4% in January, also a little more than expected, reported RTTNews.com.\par \par \uc2 Livestock\par \par \uc2 Posted 12:31 -- Live cattle futures continue to remain under moderate pressure at midday with nearby losses now seen from 30 to 40 cents per cwt. The overall lack of follow-through buying activity in the complex has brought additional weakness to prices. Despite the pressure in live cattle futures, firm gains are holding in the feeder cattle market. This is allowing prices to hold 25 to 50 cents per cwt higher even though grain markets have found additional underlying support at midday. Hot trade is weak during the last couple hours of trade Wednesday as the pressure developing in the live cattle futures market has spilled over to the hog market and bringing additional uncertainty to the complex.\par \par \uc2 Posted 11:01 -- Live cattle futures eased lower midmorning, although trading ranges remain in a narrow range with very little additional market direction across the entire complex. Live cattle futures are holding losses of 10 to 35 cents per cwt on an overall lack of volume in the market. Lean hog futures and feeder cattle trade remains higher, but well off early support, with prices steady to 22 cents higher in most contracts. The overall lack of trader direction is not expected to cause significant concern at this point as traders are still adjusting to the sharp gains seen Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Posted 09:27 -- Livestock futures were higher through the first hour of trade Wednesday morning, although prices have pulled back from initial levels as buyer interest slows significantly after Tuesday afternoon\u8217\'20\'19s aggressive gains. It is uncertain at this point if renewed buyer activity will develop during the morning, or if prices will remain stuck in the currently narrow trading ranges seen in the complex. Live cattle and lean hog futures are holding gains from 5 to 30 cents per cwt in nearby contracts. Feeder cattle markets continue to see slightly more support, but the intensity of trade activity is quickly evaporating and could lead to further price pressure over the next few hours.\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020170216ed2g00031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
