\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's real strengthens as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  resumes  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2017\par 00:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Updates chart, first paragraph)\par     By Bruno Federowski\par     SAO PAULO, Feb 15 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real firmed to\par its strongest in more than a year and a half against the dollar\par on Wednesday, while the Mexican peso weakened after U.S.\par consumer prices notched their largest gain in nearly four years.\par     The real extended gains for a second day following a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decision to resume \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , closing\par up 0.95 percent at 3.065 per dollar, its strongest since June\par 18, 2015.\par     Brazil's benchmark Bovespa stock index rose 1.9\par percent. The MSCI EM emerging market stocks index\par earlier hit a 19-month high.\par     Mexico's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slipped 0.16 percent to close at\par 20.28 per greenback after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Labor Department\f28 \uc2 \f2  said its\par Consumer Price Index jumped 0.6 percent last month, surpassing\par analyst expectations of a 0.3 percent increase.\par     The figures fueled expectations the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will\par raise U.S. interest rates as soon as next month, despite\par uncertainty over President Donald Trump's policies.\par     Mexico's benchmark IPC stock index closed down 0.5\par percent.\par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 2230 GMT:\par  Stock indexes                      daily %     