\par\par \b \uc2 Amid Trump selloff, Brazil turnaround story outshines Mexico\b0\par\par\uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2016\par 21:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO/NEW YORK, Nov 28 (Reuters) - U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's surprise victory could be a blessing in disguise for Brazil as the country's agenda of fiscal reform and low reliance on trade lure investors away from more vulnerable Mexican markets.\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets have sold off since the Nov. 8 election on concerns tax cuts and heavy infrastructure spending under a Trump administration could force the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to hike rates faster, potentially draining capital from high-yielding assets in the developing world.\par \par \uc2 Many also fear a global trade shock if Trump makes good on campaign pledges to review trade accords.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real tumbled 8 percent in the four days following the vote, the second-worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Latin America behind the Mexican peso. But the real has since stabilized to around 3.40 to the dollar as the initial shock faded.\par \par \uc2 Investors say optimism over President \uc2 Michel Temer\uc2 's reform agenda has lifted foreign direct investment in Brazil and left it less exposed to market volatility in the wake of Trump's win.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's relatively closed economy, as well as its status as a net seller of oil, make it an attractive alternative to Mexico, which sells about 80 percent of its exports to the United States. Concerns over Mexico's budget and economy have also greyed the skies of the former market darling.\par \par \uc2 Many investors have been pivoting from Mexico to Brazil since at least July, according to a Reuters survey of fund managers, a trend that could accelerate throughout the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Steve Tananbaum, founder of \uc2 GoldenTree Asset Management\uc2 , said he is "quite constructive" on Brazil and Argentina, where right-leaning administrations have taken office in the past year with agendas for business-friendly reforms.\par \par \uc2 "Their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are down precipitously yet we think there are a lot of positive changes going on there," he said. "Politically, they both have had changes in leadership that are pro-growth administrations."\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL VS ARGENTINA\par \par \uc2 Even after the recent selloff, the Brazilian real and the Argentine peso remain among the world's best performing assets this year, supported by the policies of Brazil's Temer and Argentina's \uc2 Mauricio Macri\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both took over from leftist predecessors who sought to stimulate the economy with loose purse strings and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures.\par \par \uc2 Their belt-tightening efforts have captivated investors, though \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's roughly $2 trillion economy has yet to rebound from its deepest recession in decades.\par \par \uc2 Years of protectionism have slashed both economies' reliance on foreign trade, sheltering them from potential shocks.\par \par \uc2 Still, Argentina remains vulnerable to sudden capital outflows, having just returned to international debt markets after years of legal wrangling, investors said.\par \par \uc2 "If there is broader risk aversion in the markets that prevents Argentina from entering and raising the funds that it needs then Argentina becomes vulnerable," Siobhan Morden, head of Latin American fixed-income strategy at \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL HAS "FAT TO BURN"\par \par \uc2 While bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slumped, emerging market stocks have been mixed since the election as prospects of infrastructure spending boosted prices of industrial metals.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's benchmark Bovespa stock index fell 3.8 percent since the vote, in comparison to a 6.2 percent slide in Mexican equities, while an index tracking shares of basic product producers rose 13.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Carlos Sequeira, head of research at \uc2 Banco BTG Pactual SA\uc2 , said Brazilian stocks, which are up 45 percent in 2016, could rise even further if Temer manages to gather lawmaker support for measures to curb public spending and limit debt growth.\par \par \uc2 That would allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue its rate-cutting cycle, he said, making riskier stocks more attractive in relative terms.\par \par \uc2 The fact that real rates are higher in Brazil than in most of its Latin American peers suggests there would be room for rate cuts even if a weaker real fostered price pressures.\par \par \uc2 "There is fat to burn even if U.S. rates rise more than expected," Sequeira said. (Reporting by Bruno Federowski and Dion Rabouin; Editing by Andrew Hay) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-28T21:43:23.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161128ecbs00znf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
