\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-\uc2 Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  to stick with gradual rate cut plan -governor\b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2016\par 16:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Goldfajn comments, background on economy)\par \par \par \par \uc2 By Alonso Soto\par \par \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Nov 16 (Reuters) - Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Ilan Goldfajn said on Wednesday  policymakers will stick to their plan of gradually reducing interest rates despite the market selloff triggered by the United States' election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the White House.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Last week's surprise victory of the businessman and reality TV star has jolted emerging markets, which worry his promises to inject more stimulus into the U.S. economy could force the Fed to increase interest rates more than expected.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The real has lost nearly 8 percent of its value in its worst four-day stretch since 2011, forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the sale of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, or derivatives that mimic future dollar sales.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Goldfajn said there has been no talk of joint action with other central banks to ease the market turmoil and he expects the volatility to subside once investors learn more details of  Trump's agenda.\par \par \par \par \uc2 "Regarding monetary policy, the message of the last meeting continues to be valid," Goldfajn told reporters on a conference call.\par \par \par \par \uc2 For the first time in four years, the bank cut its benchmark Selic rate by 25 basis points to 14 percent on Oct. 19, saying a gradual and moderate monetary easing was compatible with its target to bring inflation down to 4.5 percent next year.\par \par \par \par \uc2 The bank kept the Selic steady for over a year to battle inflation, ignoring calls from business groups and politicians to cut some of the world's highest interest rates.\par \par \par \par \uc2 A weaker real in the wake of Trump's election has reduced investors' bets for a more aggressive rate cut at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next monetary policy meeting on Nov 29.    (Editing by Jeffrey Benkoe and Matthew Lewis)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-19T15:41:43.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161116ecbg00rxl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
