\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil real snaps a 4-day losing streak on stepped-up  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2016\par 16:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Nov 16 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real firmed on Wednesday as government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  snap a four-day losing streak triggered by U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's surprise victory.\par \par \uc2 The real rallied as much as 1.1 percent before trimming gains to trade 0.5 percent higher at 3.42 reais, buoyed by steps taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the National Treasury to stanch the sell-off.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Treasury are doing their best to show they're ready to act if markets go haywire," said Paulo Nepomuceno, head of fixed-income trading at Coinvalores brokerage.\par \par \uc2 "That is helping brighten investor sentiment after four days of panic throughout Brazilian markets," he added.\par \par \uc2 The cost to annually insure Brazilian debt against default for five years, narrowed to 309 basis points on Wednesday from 329 basis points on Monday ahead of a local market holiday on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled following Trump's Nov. 8 election on concerns that his pledges of heavy infrastructure spending and tax cuts could drive the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to increase interest rates faster than previously expected. A rise in U.S. rates could drain funds away from higher-yielding emerging market assets.\par \par \uc2 Traders also feared a global trade shock as he has promised to review terms of trade accords the United States is part of.\par \par \uc2 The real fell 7.9 percent after the Nov. 8 election through Monday, its steepest four-day fall in five years.\par \par \uc2 The sell-off led \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step up market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling almost $4 billion worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, which function like selling dollars to investors for future delivery, from Friday through Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Nearly $1.5 billion of the swaps were new issues. The remaining $2.5 billion of the contracts will roll over some of the $6.5 billion in swaps maturing in December.\par \par \uc2 The bank also halted daily sales of reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, equivalent to future dollar purchases.\par \par \uc2 After the market close on Monday, the National Treasury announced it would also take action to stem market volatility, offering to repurchase real-denominated federal bonds between Wednesday and Friday and halting the auction of new bonds this week.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury repurchased 445,300 fixed-rate coupon-bearing reais bonds known as NTN-Fs on Wednesday, out of a total offer of 1 million bonds.\par \par \uc2 Yields on fixed-rate bonds and rate future contracts <0#2DIJ:> fell after widening sharply over the last four trading days.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Bruno Federowski; Editing by W Simon) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-16T16:35:17.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161116ecbg00rmi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
