\par\par \b \uc2 Fed Overshadowing Election for Investors -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2016\par 18:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 12:11 ET - With markets focused on the Fed in recent weeks, the lack of attention on the presidential election is striking some in the market as odd. Bob Haberkorn, senior market strategist at RJO Futures, says most of the election chatter centers around what it means for Fed policy rather than how each candidate will impact markets. While analysts have been speculating on how the results will affect certain asset classes, Haberkorn notes clients have generally been uninterested in positioning for the election--unlike previous years. "It doesn't feel like we have a presidential election coming up. I don't think the markets are really weighing in on the election outcome." (stephanie.yang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1602 GMT Supply in the euro high yield market is picking up this month but investors have come out of September with enough cash to absorb it, according to Olivier Monnoyeur, high yield portfolio manager at BNP Paribas. Cash balances have gone up during the summer, as money returned to investors in the form of coupons, bond maturities and early redemptions. And replacing redeeming bonds with new issues is difficult in August, Mr. Monnoyeur adds. In addition, many recent high yield bonds were launched to refinance existing debt, as was the case with \uc2 eDreams Odigeo\uc2 , Intralot and Avis. Therefore, net supply is lower than the new bonds' nominal amount. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:27 ET - The Brazilian real could strengthen to 2.80/dollar in the next few months as the country's high interest rates attract cash being poured into markets by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stimulus, according to Pedro Paulo Silveira, chief economist at the Nova Futura brokerage in Sao Paulo. He adds share prices of Brazilian companies are also low, in historical terms, and should attract investors' dollars as well. Silveira believes as much as $50B could find its way into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in coming months and that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market won't be enough to keep the dollar from sliding against the real. The dollar is at BRL3.21, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, after starting 2016 at BRL3.96. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1458 GMT Cover ratios at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2 's long-dated QE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are still lower compared to purchases in shorter maturities but long sterling corporate bond issuance should to create a virtuous circle. \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  anticipates opportunistic supply from public and private sector issuers looking to tap into investors who seek yield pick-up versus gilts. Stimulating new issuance of corporate bonds is one of the aims the BOE's credit QE scheme, which has no maximum maturity limit. When long supply picks up, investors will be more willing to sell long-dated gilts to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adds \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1447 GMT After three straight months of decline, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2 's measure of consumer confidence in the eurozone rose in September, albeit very slightly, to minus 8.2 from minus 8.5. Economists had expected the measure to be unchanged. Signs of stabilization suggest consumer spending may not slow much further after an easing in the second quarter, but the measure remains well below its most recent peaks of early 2015 and doesn't augur a return to more robust rates of economic expansion. (paul.hannon@wsj.com , @PaulHannon29) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:31 ET - In its new quarterly forecast, economists at TD Bank push back the timing in which \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  begins raising rates to late 2018, or another two-plus years. TD Bank's outlook also suggests GDP growth in Canada will remain at below 2% in 2017 and 2018, at 1.8% and 1.7% respectively. It said it expects meager 1.1% advance this year. The long-awaited rotation to nonenergy exports and business investment should resume in the coming quarters, as indicators suggest a resumption in US business investment and inventory accumulation. It warns though about uncertainty fueled by US election and warns spending in the energy sector will only begin to "modestly" increase in 2018. Subdued forecast emerges after speech from BoC Gov. Poloz saying rates to stay low for extended period. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:30 ET - The pickup in Mexican inflation in early September, coupled with the recent slide in the peso, could prompt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to raise interest rates next week, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2 . It had been expecting a half-point boost for December, "but we now think there is a good chance that this rate increase is brought forward to this month's meeting." CPI rose 0.5% the first half of this month, pushing the annual rate to 2.9% from 2.7% in August. Core CPI rose 0.4%, or 3.05% on-year; the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's CPI target is 3%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:17 ET - The median US worker had been with his or her current employer for 4.2 years as of January. That's according to the Labor Department's biennial report on employee tenure. The median worker tenure had slowly but steadily increased since the turn of the century, rising from 3.5 years in 2000 to 3.7 in 2002, 4.0 in 2004 and 2006, 4.1 in 2008, 4.4 in 2010 and 4.6 in 2012 and 2014. This was the first decline recorded since the figure slipped in 2000 from 1998's 3.6 years. Older Americans, public-sector workers and men generally have longer tenure than other employees. (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com; @BenLeubsdorf)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:05 ET - Existing-home sales fell for the second straight month in August, a reflection of tight inventories and rising prices. Sales were down 0.9% from the prior month to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 5.33 million, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Association of Realtors\f28 \uc2 \f2  said. Prices, meanwhile, have gained 5.1% from a year earlier and the total housing inventory has slipped 10.1% from a year earlier. Year-over-year prices have climbed for 54 consecutive months and inventories have fallen for 15 consecutive months. "Supply and affordability restrictions continue to keep too many would-be buyers on the sidelines," NAR Chief Economist Lawrence Yun says. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com; @jeffsparshott)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:04 ET - Brazil won't be able to save taxpayer money to pay down its ballooning debt for years, forecasts BMI Research, saying if Congress passes fiscal reform the federal primary budget deficit (which excludes interest payments) will narrow to around 3% of GDP by 2020 from last year's 10%. It ran surpluses from the late 1990s through 2013. BMI says the country's economy has hit bottom and tax revenue should increase next year, helping reduce the primary deficit to around 8.9% of GDP. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1403 GMT "Don't be fooled by current calmness," warns Deutsche Asset Management, as Brexit could again become a market mover once the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.K. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  decides to trigger Article 50 and begin the formal exit process. However, gilts are set to remain at low levels, says the asset manager. Government bonds are seen as haven assets, so market volatility should reinforce gilt flows. Deutsche doesn't see Article 50 being invoked before spring 2017. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2 's GBP60 billion boost to its gilt purchase program, announced in August, will have ended by then - unless it is extended. (tasos.vossos@wsj.com, @tasosvos) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1403 GMT GBP trades sharply higher, buoyed after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  policymaker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kristin Forbes\f28 \uc2 \f2  said she sees no need for more monetary stimulus. GBP/USD jumps 0.6% to be above $1.31, hitting a week high at $1.3117. EUR/GBP falls 0.1% to 0.8576. Sterling is also helped against a broadly weaker dollar after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  left interest rates unchanged on Wednesday. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 22, 2016 12:11 ET (16:11 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160922ec9m002nb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
