\par\par \b \uc2 US and Europe at Odds Over Setting Some Aviation Standards -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,318 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2016\par 17:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11:37 ET - US and European technical standard-setting organizations are headed in different directions when it comes to two fast-changing areas of aviation: allocation of certain radio frequencies for specific ground-to-air uses and increasing pilot reliance on data available on laptops, or what are known as electronic flight bags. Technical-advisory groups from both sides of the Atlantic are scheduled to meet in Boston later this year to try to resolve the friction. But for now, Europeans are proposing to develop specific standards for the types of uses appropriate to such laptops. Their US counterparts seek to leave those decisions entirely to industry. At the same time, US and European experts aren't coordinating on radio-frequency issues. (andy.pasztor@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:30 ET - Match's (MTCH) Tinder is investing in a social-network app which helps women make friends. Hey! VINA is one of 2 friend-finding apps that's grown rapidly this year. For Tinder, the investment is a step towards broadening the types of introductions it facilitates beyond romance, something CEO Sean Rad has said he is interested in. (georgia.wells@wsj.com; @georgia_wells)\par \par \uc2 11:30 ET - \uc2 The International Air Transport Association\uc2 , the global trade group, says it applied for antitrust immunity with the US Transportation Department so airlines can jointly discuss options to maintain flight connections to troubled Venezuela in light of that government's refusal to release $3.8B in airline revenues. Caracas's approvals to repatriate funds have slowed to a trickle and airlines have been forced to cut their flights to the country as a result. According to \uc2 IATA\uc2 , international airline capacity to and from Venezuela has plunged 61% from 2013, and the number of passengers has dwindled, further hurting the local economy. The DOT had no immediate comment on the unusual request. (susan.carey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:27 ET - The Brazilian real could strengthen to 2.80/dollar in the next few months as the country's high interest rates attract cash being poured into markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus, according to Pedro Paulo Silveira, chief economist at the Nova Futura brokerage in Sao Paulo. He adds share prices of Brazilian companies are also low, in historical terms, and should attract investors' dollars as well. Silveira believes as much as $50B could find its way into \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months and that \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market won't be enough to keep the dollar from sliding against the real. The dollar is at BRL3.21, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, after starting 2016 at BRL3.96. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:23 ET - \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  (TMUS) is putting a positive spin on announcements from \uc2 Comcast\uc2  (CMCSA) and Charter (CHTR) to begin experiments in offering wireless service. Firstly, their success would mean six major wireless carriers instead of the current four and TMUS implies that could mean it gets acquired. They contend cable companies are operating on old agreements to run over \uc2 Verizon\uc2 's (VZ) network and also may find the industry is too tough for them to really succeed. In the end, they highlighted the delay until these services are available. "Things in cable happen at the pace of cable," TMUS COO \uc2 Mike Sievert\uc2  says at a \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  conference. TMUS up 0.7% at $46.20.(thomas.gryta@wsj.com; @tgryta)\par \par \uc2 10:59 ET - Mizuho worries \uc2 Red Hat\uc2 's (RHT) 2Q results may not be "as strong as they initially appear." The Linux-focused software company reports a 19% gain in revenue, but much of the upside came from \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related gains and "increased customer auditing activity," analyst Abhey Lamba says. He explains $3M in revenue was due to increased charges at a customer that RHT determined was using more services than its license stipulated. Lamba, who maintains a buy on RHT, also points out that two of its top 25 customers in the quarter did not renew. RHT up 5% at $80.86. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:56 ET - \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  (TMUS) executives downplay the iPhone 7 launch being a signal of increased competition in the wireless industry. The carrier, which started the latest volley of giveaways, is repeating a launch promotion and it's lasting just a week and a half, ending Sunday. Officials note the bulk of any benefits from people switching to TMUS for Apple's (AAPL) new product won't be seen until 4Q because of supply scarcity and not counting an added line until the new phone ships. (thomas.gryta@wsj.com; @tgryta)\par \par \uc2 10:53 ET - Natural-gas futures turn lower despite a storage addition that was roughly in line with expectations. What gives? Traders were betting on a lower number, says Kyle Cooper, a consultant for ION Energy. "The money was bet on a mid-40s, not something with a 5-handle." Supplies rose 52B cubic feet. Still, Cooper notes the storage addition was "on an absolute basis still very bullish." After yesterday's 20-month closing high, \uc2 Nymex\uc2  October futures are down 1% at $3.06/MMBtu. (alison.sider@wsj.com; @alyrose)\par \par \uc2 10:49 ET - Global capacity to refine oil is set to grow 17% to 115.2M barrels/day by 2020, says GlobalData. But a few planned refinery projects have been canceled in the US and Bangladesh, lowering GlobalData's outlook from its February estimate that capacity would reach 118.2M then. China and Nigeria have the most planned new capacity and are expected to spend $84B on expansions the next 4 years. (nicole.friedman@wsj.com; @NicoleFriedman)\par \par \uc2 10:47 ET - \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's increased oil production the past 2 years has displaced Russian supplies in some markets, says PVM. The brokerage notes the country's share of the global oil market has fallen a touch while \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's has risen 2.3% since November 2014. "This rivalry is likely to continue even if there is an agreement to freeze production. Such a deal would merely preserve the current bearish balance." (nicole.friedman@wsj.com; @NicoleFriedman)\par \par \uc2 10:45 ET - North Carolina will have at least one Affordable Care Act exchange insurer in each county, ending worries about a possible gap. \uc2 Blue Cross and Blue Shield of North Carolina\uc2 , which has lost money on its ACA plans, has decided to continue participating in the marketplace throughout the state. The exchange there is losing \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  (UNH) and \uc2 Aetna\uc2  (AET), leaving \uc2 Blue Cross\uc2  as the sole option in nearly all of North Carolina; \uc2 Cigna\uc2  (CI) is expected to sell marketplace plans in the Raleigh area. "While the ACA still presents significant challenges, we're doing everything we can to help North Carolinians get the coverage they need," says \uc2 Blue Cross\uc2 . Exchange insurers around the country have been moving to narrower networks and more-restrictive plans. (anna.mathews@wsj.com; @annawmathews)\par \par \uc2 10:42 ET - The higher the share of women in top management roles, the greater the excess returns for investors, finds a new report from Credit Suisse Research Institute in confirming prior research at the think tank. Companies with women in 25% of senior roles had a compound annual growth rate of 2.8% more than the MSCI ACWI, a broad global index of firms. If a majority were female-filled, outperformace was above 10%. The institute looked at gender representation and financial performance at almost 3,400 companies across all industries and countries. Women represented 15% of corporate members globally at the end of 2015, up from 13% in 2013, while for senior management growth was to 14% from 13%. Looking at an identical dataset, share of female execs was essentially unchanged. (lauren.weber@wsj.com; @laurenweberWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2016 11:37 ET (15:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160922ec9m002h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
