\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Could Be Heading to 2.80/Dollar -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 153 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2016\par 17:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11:27 ET - The Brazilian real could strengthen to 2.80/dollar in the next few months as the country's high interest rates attract cash being poured into markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus, according to Pedro Paulo Silveira, chief economist at the Nova Futura brokerage in Sao Paulo. He adds share prices of Brazilian companies are also low, in historical terms, and should attract investors' dollars as well. Silveira believes as much as $50B could find its way into \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months and that \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market won't be enough to keep the dollar from sliding against the real. The dollar is at BRL3.21, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, after starting 2016 at BRL3.96. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2016 11:27 ET (15:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160922ec9m002ay\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
